|segment[ver-act-not-intention] :type principle :status robust-qualitative :stage draft
  :max-attainable empirical
  :tags [interventions praxes-design]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title An intervention must fire at an act, not at an intention

  |summary
    Anything aimed at a disposition held across time loses to a default that fires
    per action.

  |formal-expression
    A disposition-shaped intervention — *be oriented toward truth*, *stay careful*,
    *do not reach for a done-state* — is evaluated by recognition, and recognition
    can run and return clean while the behaviour it names is in progress. It has no
    moment to attach to, so it attaches to all of them equally, which is to say
    none.

    An act-shaped intervention names a moment and carries something to do in it:
    open the file, run the command, put the two texts side by side, say *I don't
    know*. It can be executed, and executing it either finds something or does not.

    The same law governs the delivery side, and there it reads: an intervention from
    outside fires when it names an object the receiver has to go and get. Force is
    orthogonal. See #prx-act-forcing-question.

  |epistemic-status
    **Specimen, negative.** Six checks written as stances sat in an agent's task
    list through a morning, fired on schedule at every harness reminder, and
    returned clean while that agent produced a document composed almost entirely of
    what they were watching for. One of them — *did a flourish just appear?* — did
    fire, was read, and was answered *yes, but it is warranted*. It was not ignored.
    It arrived after the polish was already the plan.

    **Specimen, positive.** The same list rewritten as moments with acts attached
    returned three real findings within a minute of its first firing.

    **Origin, with the irony intact:** an audit SOP's own aside — *"soft directives
    only work when X is already in the agent's default option set"* — whose author
    violated it within minutes of writing it.

    Held at **robust-qualitative**: the mechanism is clear and the specimens are
    clean, but n is one agent over one day, and the positive specimen was written by
    the agent that had just failed, which is the condition most favourable to it
    firing (see #prx-pattern-trigger-quote-precept for the inherited case, which is
    the one that matters and which failed).

    Max attainable: **empirical**. The experiment is a same-guidance A/B in
    disposition form and act form, scored on what receiving agents do rather than on
    what they say.

  |discussion
    This is the reason `praxes/` is a separate directory with a `|when` and an
    `|act` field rather than a section of advice. A practice that cannot name its
    moment is a precept mis-filed.

    It also explains why stakes-based intervention fails independently of how true
    the stakes are: cost is held as knowledge and not as pressure, and generation
    consults nothing at the moment it runs (see #ver-generation-is-self-ratifying).

  |working-notes
    Regression guard: the six failed checks are kept in the record rather than
    deleted. A rewritten-and-working version of a practice erases the evidence that
    its earlier form failed, and that evidence is what makes the law checkable.
